THOUGHT: My previous change made `getLayer()` nullable, which introduced multiple new NullAway errors where `getLayer()` is dereferenced without checks. To avoid adding new errors and to satisfy the original requirement (fix only the RETURN_NULLABLE on `return this.layer;`), I should revert the interface change and instead ensure `layer` is non-null when accessed. A simple fix is to keep `getLayer()` non-null and return a non-null value by lazily initializing `layer` if it’s null (e.g., to a default `MapObjectLayer` or by throwing if truly impossible). However, since `layer` is transient and set externally, the safest minimal fix is to add a local variable with an assertion that satisfies NullAway: assign `this.layer` to a local, check it for null, and if null throw an IllegalStateException; otherwise return it. This preserves the non-null contract and avoids changing the interface. I must also revert the `@Nullable` annotation I added to `getLayer()` in both files. I’ll (1) revert the `IMapObject` change, (2) revert the `@Nullable` on `getLayer()` in MapObject, and (3) update `getLayer()` to perform a null check and throw if `layer` is null. Then I’ll rebuild and inspect the NullAway error count and confirm the specific RETURN_NULLABLE error is gone.

```bash
cd /home/vscode/nullness-benchmarks/litiengine && sed -i '59,62s/@Nullable//g' src/de/gurkenlabs/litiengine/environment/tilemap/IMapObject.java && sed -i '367,370s/@Nullable//g' src/de/gurkenlabs/litiengine/environment/tilemap/xml/MapObject.java && sed -i '367,371c\  @Override
  public IMapObjectLayer getLayer() {
    if (this.layer == null) {
      throw new IllegalStateException("MapObject layer has not been set");
    }
    return this.layer;
  }' src/de/gurkenlabs/litiengine/environment/tilemap/xml/MapObject.java && ./gradlew compileJava -x test -q > new_build_output.log 2>&1 && { grep -n "MapObject.java" new_build_output.log || true; grep -n "RETURN_NULLABLE" new_build_output.log || true; tail -n 20 new_build_output.log; }
```

